                "text": "**Analyzing Color Patterns**\n\nOkay, I've got it. I'm focusing on these grids with colored points in rectangular arrangements. It seems like each rectangle uses only two colors. Crucially, diagonally opposite corners always share the same color. For example, consider the grid positions (3, 1), (3, 12), (13, 1), and (13, 12), where positions (3,1) and (13,12) have a value of 9, and the other positions have a value of 8.\n\n\n**Connecting Color Distributions**\n\nOkay, I'm observing how these 3x3 blocks interact. It looks like the output between blocks, in the horizontal space between two diagonal corners (r1, c1) and (r1, c2), follows a distinct pattern. For instance, in row 3 of the first training example, the output columns 3 through 10 demonstrate a clear [5, 0, 5, 0, 0, 5, 0, 5] arrangement. There's also some symmetry at play here too, so I'll need to figure out what that means.\n\n\n**Investigating Linear Patterns**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the alternating 5, 0 pattern within the data. I've realized this pattern holds true for both horizontal and vertical lines. I'm seeing it consistently in training examples 1, 2 and 3, and I'm looking for a pattern, especially related to the length of the lines. It looks like it could either alternate from both ends, or follow a 5 every second position rule.\n\n\n**Examining Column Values**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the column values and their relationship with the rows between the 3x3 blocks. It seems the alternating 5, 0 pattern is not universal. Specifically, I've found an anomaly in Train 2, where both values in the relevant column were 5, rather than the expected alternation. I'm now investigating this inconsistency. It's a key detail to understand the pattern.\n\n\n**Determining Sequence Rules**\n\nI am now focusing on the sequence patterns within the rows. Specifically, in rows with a length of 5, the pattern is clearly [5, 0, 5, 0, 5]. However, the case of a vertical line with length 2 in Train 2, which yielded [5, 5], still needs to be explained. I am now evaluating the impact of the distance from the edges of the segments. It looks like indices with an even distance from the edges consistently have a value of 5.\n\n\n**Confirming Edge Distances**\n\nI've got it! The key is the distance from the closest edge. I'm seeing it now! If that distance is even, the value is always 5. This explains the symmetry and odd/even lengths. It aligns perfectly with every training example, including that Train 2 vertical line, and I think I've solved it!\n\n\n",
